From 534e80e3c179c32ece6b737b5fc89cd80163cb4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christian F.K. Schaller" <christian.schaller@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:04:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] scanner: Find scanners with a helper process that asks
 libsane

This commit replaces the stand-in list of scanners with the real thing.
A new program, xdg-desktop-portal-scanner-helper, runs in its own
process and asks libsane for the devices, so that libsane and the
drivers it loads, which block and sometimes crash, stay out of the
portal. For now it has one mode, list, which writes the scanners to
standard output as a GVariant of type a(ssa{sv}): an opaque id, the
SANE device name and the information that applications get. The SANE
name stays inside the portal, since some backends read it as a path or
an address, and applications only ever see the id.

The frontend runs the helper for SelectScanners, so the dialog lists
what is there, and for ListScanners only when the application has been
granted something. Probing can take seconds and some backends reset the
scanner they find, so an application with no grants does not cause one.
The helper gets 30 seconds, its output is checked for type and encoding
before it is used, and a failure gives an empty list and a warning.
XDP_SCANNER_HELPER overrides the path, for tests.

Webcams from SANE's v4l backend are left out. The connection and the
capabilities are worked out from the device name and SANE's device type
string, as hints only.

The scanner portal is only offered when libsane is present, through a
new sane build option that is auto by default, like geoclue and gudev.
XDG_DESKTOP_PORTAL_SCANNER_FAKE=1 still gives two made-up scanners, for
trying the dialog without drivers.

Tested with the helper on its own against SANE's test backend, and
through the frontend, the helper and the xdg-desktop-portal-gnome
dialog on a private bus: the dialog listed the two test devices, the
choice came back with libsane's vendor and model, and a second
connection saw no grants. Not tested: the helper timeout, a helper that
fails, and that an application without grants causes no probe.
